<worksheet>
  <title>LT16  Quadratic Applications</title>
  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>As a group, discuss what each question is asking, and what the independent and dependent variables are.
        Then set up the equation and solve it. Be sure to answer in a complete sentence that includes the value(s) you found and their meaning in the context of the problem.
        Before moving on, compare your answers and make sure everyone in your group understands the problem and the solution.</p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>Juan is looking to install sod in the back yard of a newly built house. The rectangular area is <m>160\text{ m}^2</m>. Determine the length and width of the land to place the new sod lawn if the length is <m>x+3</m> m and the width is <m>x-3</m> m.</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>One side of a rectangle is 5 meters longer than the other. If the area of the rectangle is 60 square meters, find the dimensions of the rectangle.</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>You are going to be comparing the motion of two people jumping into a pool from the same height.
        Fred flops into a pool from a platform that is 18.75 feet above the water. His height above the water, in feet, after <m>t</m> seconds is given by the equation <m>F(t) = -16t^2 + 18.75</m>.
        Gina flops into the same pool from the same platform. Her height above the water, in feet, after <m>t</m> seconds is given by the equation <m>G(t) = -5(t-0.5)^2+20</m>.</p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>What is the highest height that Fred reaches? When does he reach it?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>How long will it take for Fred to hit the water?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>What is the highest height that Gina reaches? When does she reach it?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>How long will it take for Gina to hit the water?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>